<item>
  <title>Designing a simulation</title>
  <dc:creator>Navdeep Rana</dc:creator>
  <link>https://navdeeprana.github.io/blog/000-designing-a-simulation.html</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<section id="introduction" class="level1">
<h1>Introduction</h1>
</section>
<section id="model" class="level1">
<h1>Model</h1>
<p>As a test case, we will consider the FKPP equation</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cbegin%7Balign%7D%0A%5Cpartial_t%20u%20=%20D%20%5Cnabla%5E2%20u%20+%20%5Cgamma%20u%20%5Cleft(1%20-%20%5Cfrac%7Bu%7D%7BZ%7D%20%5Cright),%0A%5Cend%7Balign%7D"> where <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?u"> is a density field, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?D"> is the diffusion coefficient, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cgamma"> is the time scale governing the growth rate and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Z"> is the carrying capacity. It is ideal to convert the dimensional equation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ceqref%7Beq:fkpp%7D"> into a dimensionless form by using the following transformations <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cbegin%7Balign%7D%0Au'%20%5Crightarrow%20Z%20u,~t'%20%5Crightarrow%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cgamma%7D%20t,~l'%20%5Crightarrow%20%5Csqrt%7B%5Cfrac%7BD%7D%7B%5Cgamma%7D%7D%20l,%0A%5Cend%7Balign%7D"> which gives us the following equation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cbegin%7Balign%7D%0A%5Cpartial_t%20u%20=%20%5Cnabla%5E2%20u%20+%20u%20%5Cleft(1%20-%20u%20%5Cright)%20%5Clabel%7Beq:fkpp%7D,%0A%5Cend%7Balign%7D"> where we have ignored prime indices for brevity. Surprisingly, with proper choice of units, there are no free parameters left in <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ceqref%7Beq:fkpp%7D">.</p>
</section>
<section id="elements-of-a-numerical-simulation" class="level1">
<h1>Elements of a numerical simulation</h1>
<p>A typical numerical simulation of a partial differential equation consists of the following parts</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>A grid or a domain</strong> on which the equation lives in. Our analytical equation is valid for a certain domain with particular boundary conditions. The grid is the discretized translation of the same. In liu of simplicity, we are going to assume a Cartesian grid with periodic or dirichlet boundary conditions.</p></li>
<li><p>Variables</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Parameters</strong> Given an equation in its dimensional or dimensionless form, the parameters, for example <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?D,~%5Cgamma,Z"> for <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ceqref%7Beq:fkpp%7D"> or no parameters for <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ceqref%7Beq:fkpp%7D"> along with initial and boundary conditions determine its evolution. Typically, parameters stay constant throughout a simultion. We can either use a <code>struct</code> or a set of <code>NamedTuple</code> to define these parameters. For example we could do something like</p>
<div class="code-copy-outer-scaffold"><div class="sourceCode" id="cb1" style="background: #f1f3f5;"><pre class="sourceCode julia code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode julia"><span id="cb1-1">p <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> (D <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="fl" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">1</span>., γ <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="fl" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">1</span>., Z <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="fl" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">1</span>.)</span></code></pre></div></div></li>
<li><p>A time stepper, which takes care of numerically integrating the equations</p></li>
<li><p>Housekeeping routines, which do grunt work</p></li>
<li><p>Input/Output and data analysis.</p></li>
</ul>
<section id="grid" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="grid">Grid</h2>
<p>We begin by creating an abstract grid type with one type parameter, which defines the precision of data residing on the grid. In our case, we are dealing with a real density field so our data could be a <code>Float</code>. However, for different physical models the precision or base data type could be different. For example, for Ising model, the field is a Spin field, which is easily represented by <code>Bool</code> or a <code>Int8</code>.</p>
<div class="code-copy-outer-scaffold"><div class="sourceCode" id="cb2" style="background: #f1f3f5;"><pre class="sourceCode julia code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode julia"><span id="cb2-1"><span class="kw" style="color: #003B4F;
background-color: null;
font-weight: bold;
font-style: inherit;">abstract type</span> AbstractGrid{T} <span class="kw" style="color: #003B4F;
background-color: null;
font-weight: bold;
font-style: inherit;">end</span></span></code></pre></div></div>
<p>What makes up a grid? Well we need to know its extents, the step size, the range of x, y values possible. You can ask, what happened to the boundary conditions, and I would argue that it is a property of the data/variables that reside on the grid, not the grid itself so we take care of it while handling data</p>
</section>
</section>
<section id="variables" class="level1">
<h1>Variables</h1>
<p>Variables are what make up the simulation.</p>


</section>

 ]]></description>
